# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: mock-CAD DRIVE-PROTOCOL-ACTIONS test family flakes on the native Windows lane
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: test-determinism
#+DATE: 2026-09-28

* Observed

=test:alfe:windows= (native Windows runner, allow_failure) intermittently
fails a shifting subset of the mock-CAD protocol / dribble tests. TWO runs of
the SAME commit failed DIFFERENT tests:

- run A (job 16772780938): =CAD-DRIVE-PROTOCOL-ACTIONS-THREE-EVALS-COUNTER-AWARE=
  (got =:ABORTED=, expected =:SUCCESS=) and
  =CLAUTOLISP-SUBPROCESS-EVAL-PARITY-WITH-DIRECT=.
- run B (retry 16773377643): =CAD-DRIVE-PROTOCOL-ACTIONS-INTERACTIVE-LOOP-ROUNDTRIPS=,
  =CAD-DRIVE-PROTOCOL-ACTIONS-END-TO-END= (x2),
  =ALFE-DRIBBLE-RECORDS-A-WHOLE-MOCK-CAD-SESSION=.

A different member of the same family loses each run -> a timing race, not a
deterministic defect. The SAME alfe code passed =test:alfe:windows= clean on a
master pipeline a few hours earlier (2888394131, alfe 2.2.141), so it is
runner-load dependent. The Linux =test:alfe:sbcl= lane is green throughout.

* Relation to prior work

=issues/closed/alfe-windows-drive-protocol-three-evals-fails.issue= fixed the
=THREE-EVALS= =:ABORTED= symptom (per-round budget 2.2.122; probe-then-open
transient 2.2.125) and observed it green on the lane 2026-09-27. Its own
resolution note warned it was "not proven this was the LAST cause". This ticket
is that caveat coming true, and BROADER: the whole DRIVE-PROTOCOL-ACTIONS +
mock-CAD + dribble family, not one test.

* Cause (hypothesis)

The mock CAD and the drive-protocol harness coordinate through the file-based
protocol (status.txt / stdin.txt polling) with per-round time budgets. On a
loaded Windows runner the budgets are occasionally too tight and an action is
reported =:ABORTED= before the mock has serviced it -- the same class the
three-evals fix addressed, but the budget/handshake is not yet robust across
the whole family.

* Fix direction

Make the mock-CAD protocol tests deterministic rather than time-budgeted:
pre-queue the mock's replies and drive them single-threaded / step-locked so a
slow runner cannot make an action appear finished before the mock has read its
stdin, mirroring the determinism note in
=clautolisp-test-double-run-and-native-ci=. Audit the whole
=CAD-DRIVE-PROTOCOL-ACTIONS-*= + dribble family, not just one test.

* Acceptance criteria

- =test:alfe:windows= passes the DRIVE-PROTOCOL-ACTIONS / mock-CAD / dribble
  family across several consecutive runs on the loaded runner (no shifting
  subset failures).
- The determinism is structural (pre-queued / step-locked), not a larger
  timeout.
- Linux and macOS lanes remain green.

* Note

Filed per AGENTS.md while advancing the two NEXT AutoCAD triage items: the
native Windows lane flagged this on an unrelated MR (!331, probe + triage only).
Not caused by that MR nor by the accoreconsole-deported-load-highbyte fixes;
those change no mock-CAD test path.

* Implemented, alfe 2.2.145 (2026-09-28) — awaiting Windows runs

The cause was not only a budget. Reading the family end to end found a WRITE
that nothing retried, and two deadlines on the success path.

1. *The writing side of the Windows replace window had no retry.*
   WRITE-ATOMIC-FILE's overwrite is, on Windows, UIOP's delete-target then
   rename; either call fails with a sharing violation while the other party
   still holds the target open. READ-FILE-AS-STRING already retried that
   window from the reading side (its docstring even pointed at a "Windows
   note" in WRITE-ATOMIC-FILE that did not exist). The mock publishes
   RUNNING / DONE / STOPPING / STOPPED on status.txt, which the driver polls
   character by character -- so one unlucky tick signalled out of the mock's
   write, the thread died inside its guard (recorded, but no assertion in the
   family quoted it), and the driver waited out its 30 s and said :ABORTED.
   Which test lost varied with the load: the shifting subset. FIX, in
   PRODUCTION (file-protocol.lisp), since alfe's own writes of stdin.txt and
   control.txt face the same window against a real CAD: retry the rename,
   bounded (100 x 20 ms), then re-signal and delete the temp file. It catches
   ERROR, not FILE-ERROR -- CCL signals a bare SIMPLE-ERROR for a failed
   rename, measured, and the new test failed under CCL until it did.
2. *The mock's 20 s budget covered the WHOLE session* and was on the success
   path; a loaded runner could spend it before the last request. The mock is
   now STEP-LOCKED: it waits for the next request or for SHUTDOWN, with no
   budget, and +MOCK-CAD-SAFETY-NET-SECONDS+ (300) is reached only when a
   test is already broken. Its 2 s SHUTDOWN wait -- which published STOPPED
   whether or not SHUTDOWN had come -- is step-locked the same way, and the
   control.txt delete, the last bare file op in the cycle, is retried.
3. *A failing test stays fast.* %FINISH-MOCK-CAD replaces the bare
   JOIN-THREAD: when the driver stopped early it sends SHUTDOWN itself, so a
   failure no longer costs the safety net.
4. *A dead mock now says so.* Every test of the family resets
   *MOCK-CAD-CONDITION* and quotes %MOCK-CAD-FAILURE-NOTE in its :SUCCESS
   assertion, the dribble test included (it asserted nothing about the
   status before). The subprocess-parity test -- a different class: it runs
   clautolisp-sbcl synchronously and fails only on a non-zero exit -- now
   quotes the child's stdout and stderr instead of only "not :SUCCESS".

New tests: protocol-write-atomic-file-gives-up-loudly-and-cleans-its-temp (the
retry is bounded and leaves no temp; a directory stands in for an
unreplaceable target, since the transient itself cannot be arranged off
Windows) and cad-mock-stops-on-shutdown-without-its-requests (an unfed mock
stops within 10 s of SHUTDOWN -- the old 20 s budget would fail it). alfe suite
1352/1352 on SBCL and on CCL (Linux).

*Still to do before closing*: the acceptance above -- several consecutive
green test:alfe:windows runs on the loaded runner. Not claimed from Linux.
